        <p>Several factors can speed up or slow down your application. Understanding these variables helps you plan more accurately.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>1. Application Completeness (The Biggest Factor)</h3>
      <p>A complete application with all required documents, correctly translated and apostilled, can be approved in 4–6 weeks. An incomplete application automatically goes into a queue for document requests, adding 4–8 weeks minimum to your timeline. Common mistakes that cause delays include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Missing or expired documents</li>
        <li>Translations that are not certified by a sworn translator (traductor jurado)</li>
        <li>Missing apostille stamps on official documents</li>
        <li>Criminal record certificates older than 6 months</li>
        <li>Health insurance policies that don't meet Spain's requirements</li>
        <li>Bank statements or proof of income that don't cover the full required period</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. Consulate Workload and Seasonality</h3>
      <p>Consulates handle varying application volumes throughout the year. Peak seasons are typically:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>July–August:</strong> Summer holidays and vacation periods. Staff shortages can extend timelines by 2–4 weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>November–December:</strong> End-of-year holidays. Processing slows during this period.</li>
        <li><strong>January–March:</strong> Post-holiday backlog. Many applicants submit in early January, creating delays.</li>
        <li><strong>April–June:</strong> Typically fastest processing times. Lower application volumes and full staffing levels.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you have flexibility in your application timing, submit between April and June to increase the likelihood of faster processing.</p>

      <h3>3. Background Check Requirements</h3>
      <p>Consulates conduct thorough background checks on all NLV applicants. If you have lived in multiple countries, the consulate will request criminal record certificates from each jurisdiction. This process can take 4–6 weeks or longer depending on how quickly your home country and other countries issue certificates. Some countries (Australia, New Zealand, Canada, USA, UK) provide digital criminal records quickly, while others may require months of processing.</p>

      <h3>4. Income Verification Complexity</h3>
      <p>Demonstrating sufficient passive income is straightforward for some applicants (e.g., retirees with pensions) but complex for others. If your income comes from multiple sources (rental properties, investments, dividends), the consulate may request additional documentation to verify the sources and amounts. This can add 2–4 weeks to processing.</p>

      <h3>5. Health Insurance Validation</h3>
      <p>The consulate verifies that your private health insurance meets Spanish requirements. If your policy has gaps or unclear coverage, the consulate will request clarification from your insurer. Insurance companies can take 1–2 weeks to respond, extending your timeline.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Critical:</strong> Even a single missing document can restart your processing timeline. Consulates do not proceed with incomplete applications. Always submit a full checklist with your application confirming you have every required item in multiple copies (typically 3–4 sets).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Waiting period ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section" style="background: var(--cream);">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>What Happens During the NLV Visa Waiting Period?</h2>
        <p>Once you submit your application at the consulate, the waiting period begins. Here's what the consulate does during those 6–12 weeks.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Weeks 1–2: Initial Document Review</h3>
      <p>The consulate's administrative staff perform an initial review to confirm all required documents are present. If anything is missing or incorrect, they will issue a document request (either by email or mail) asking you to submit the missing items. Responding quickly to document requests is essential—delays in submitting additional documents can add 4 weeks or more to your timeline.</p>

      <h3>Weeks 2–4: Formal Application Registration</h3>
      <p>Once your file is deemed complete, the application is formally registered and assigned a case reference number. At this point, your application enters the substantive review stage. This is when you can begin tracking your application's status using your reference number.</p>

      <h3>Weeks 3–6: Background Checks and Verifications</h3>
      <p>The consulate contacts authorities in your home country and any other countries where you have lived for more than six months in the past five years to verify your criminal record. This process can take 4–6 weeks or longer depending on how quickly those countries respond. Spain also verifies your income sources with your banks or employers.</p>

      <h3>Weeks 6–8: Medical and Insurance Review</h3>
      <p>The consulate's medical advisor reviews your health insurance policy to confirm it meets Spanish requirements. They verify that your policy covers the full scope of medical care required by law and has no territorial exclusions within Spain. Your insurer may be contacted directly to confirm coverage details.</p>

      <h3>Weeks 8–10: Final Decision Preparation</h3>
      <p>Once all background checks and verifications are complete, a consulate officer prepares a formal assessment of your application. They compare your application against the legal requirements and prepare a recommendation for approval or denial.</p>

      <h3>Weeks 10–12+: Final Approval and Notification</h3>
      <p>The senior consulate official reviews the assessment and makes a final decision. Approved applicants are notified by email and/or mail with instructions for collecting their visa. Notification typically comes 1–2 weeks after the final decision.</p>

      <p>Once you receive approval notification, you have 90 days to collect your visa from the consulate and to physically enter Spain. Do not delay collecting your visa—consulates hold visa documentation for a limited time before returning it to archives.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Delayed applications ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>What to Do If Your NLV Application Is Delayed</h2>
        <p>If your application exceeds the typical processing timeframe by more than two weeks, you have several options to obtain status updates and expedite the process.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Step 1: Obtain Your Case Reference Number</h3>
      <p>When you submit your application at the consulate, immediately request and save your case reference number. This unique identifier is essential for tracking your application. Write it down and save it in multiple places. Without it, you cannot request status updates.</p>

      <h3>Step 2: Contact the Consulate Directly</h3>
      <p>If your application has been processing for longer than the stated timeframe, contact the consulate's visa department. Most consulates have a general inquiry email address and a phone line for status updates. Be polite and professional—consulate staff handle many inquiries and respond better to courteous requests. Provide your case reference number and ask for a status update.</p>

      <h3>Step 3: Use a Visa Service Provider</h3>
      <p>If you used a BLS office or other authorized visa service provider to submit your application, contact them to request a status update. Many service providers have direct relationships with consulates and can obtain information faster than individuals can. If they submitted your application on your behalf, they should be able to track its progress.</p>

      <h3>Step 4: Request an Official Status Report</h3>
      <p>If your application has been pending for more than 12 weeks without contact from the consulate, you can request a formal status report. Most consulates will provide this information if you make a written request in person, by email, or by post. This request may prompt the consulate to expedite your application if there are any bottlenecks.</p>

      <h3>Step 5: Escalate to the Spanish Embassy (If Necessary)</h3>
      <p>If your application remains unresolved after more than 14 weeks, and the consulate is unresponsive, you can escalate your complaint to the Spanish Embassy in your country. Embassies oversee consulate operations and can request status updates on problematic cases. However, use this option only as a last resort, as it can sometimes complicate your application process.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">🚨</span>
        <p><strong>Do not ignore the timeline:</strong> Remember that once your visa is approved, it remains valid for only 90 days. You must collect your visa from the consulate and physically enter Spain within this window. If you do not collect your visa within 90 days of approval, you will need to reapply.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Post-approval timeline ────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section" style="background: var(--cream);">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Post-Approval Timeline: After Your NLV Visa Is Approved</h2>
        <p>Once your Non-Lucrative Visa is approved, you enter a critical phase. The following timeline is mandatory and time-sensitive.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Days 1–90: Collect Your Visa and Enter Spain</h3>
      <p>After you receive approval notification, you have 90 days to collect your physical visa from the consulate. Most consulates require you to collect it in person, though some allow authorized representatives to collect on your behalf. Your approval letter will include specific instructions for collection.</p>
      <p>Once you receive your visa in your passport, you have the full 90-day validity window to physically enter Spain. You do not have to enter immediately—you can take up to 90 days—but you must enter before the 90-day period expires. The visa activation date is the date you cross the Spanish border, not the date of issue.</p>

      <h3>Days 0–30 After Arrival in Spain: Apply for the TIE</h3>
      <p>Within 30 days of entering Spain, you must apply for the Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero (TIE), Spain's Foreigner Identity Card. This is a <strong>legal requirement</strong>, not optional. Failure to apply within 30 days can result in fines and complications with your residency status. To apply for the TIE, you will need:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your passport and entry stamp</li>
        <li>Completed application form (EX-15), available at the National Police office</li>
        <li>Two passport-sized photos (4cm × 4cm)</li>
        <li>Proof of address in Spain (rental contract, utility bill, or certificate from your town hall)</li>
        <li>Your visa approval letter</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Submit your TIE application at your local National Police office (Policía Nacional) or through an authorized service provider (many towns have BLS offices). Processing typically takes 1–3 weeks. You will receive a provisional certificate (resguardo) immediately, which serves as temporary proof of legal status. The physical TIE card is usually ready within 2–3 weeks.</p>

      <h3>Days 0–30 After Arrival in Spain: Register on the Padrón</h3>
      <p>Simultaneously with your TIE application, you must register your address on the municipal population register (padrón) at your local town hall. The padrón is Spain's official population registry and is essential for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Accessing public healthcare through the Spanish health system</li>
        <li>Opening a Spanish bank account</li>
        <li>Obtaining residency proof for government services</li>
        <li>Demonstrating legal residence for taxes and other purposes</li>
      </ul>
      <p>To register on the padrón, visit your local town hall (ayuntamiento) with your passport, proof of address, and visa approval letter. Registration is usually completed the same day or within a few days. There is no fee for padrón registration.</p>

      <h3>Complete Timeline Summary (Entry to Full Residency Status):</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Day 1 of arrival:</strong> Collect your entry stamp</li>
        <li><strong>Days 1–7:</strong> Apply for TIE and padrón registration</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 1–3:</strong> Receive provisional TIE certificate</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 2–4:</strong> Collect physical TIE card</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 1–2:</strong> Receive padrón confirmation</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--green">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">✅</span>
        <p><strong>Once complete:</strong> After you have your TIE and padrón registration, you have full legal residency status in Spain. You can access healthcare, open bank accounts, and enjoy all the rights and benefits of a Non-Lucrative Visa resident.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Key timeline factors ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>How to Speed Up Your Non-Lucrative Visa Timeline</h2>
        <p>While you cannot control consulate processing times, you can take steps to ensure your application moves as quickly as possible through the system.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>1. Submit a 100% Complete Application</h3>
      <p>This is the single most important factor. An incomplete application automatically goes into a document-request queue, adding 4–8 weeks. Before submitting, verify that you have:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>All required documents in the correct number of copies (typically 3–4 sets)</li>
        <li>All non-English documents certified by a sworn translator (traductor jurado)</li>
        <li>All official documents apostilled</li>
        <li>Clear, legible photocopies of all documents</li>
        <li>A signed and dated checklist confirming what you are submitting</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. Submit During Off-Peak Months</h3>
      <p>If possible, submit your application between April and June when consulates experience lower application volumes. Avoid July–August and November–December, when staff vacations and holiday periods slow processing significantly.</p>

      <h3>3. Use an Authorized Visa Service Provider</h3>
      <p>Submitting through an authorized visa service (like BLS International for UK and US consulates) ensures your application is properly formatted and complete before submission. These providers are familiar with consulate requirements and can catch errors before they reach the consulate.</p>

      <h3>4. Have All Background Check Documents Ready</h3>
      <p>Obtain your criminal record certificates from all relevant countries before you start your application. Do not wait until the consulate requests them. Having these documents in hand will save 4–6 weeks of processing time.</p>

      <h3>5. Be Responsive to Document Requests</h3>
      <p>If the consulate requests additional documents or clarifications, respond within 7 days. Slow responses extend your timeline significantly. Check your email frequently during the processing period.</p>

      <h3>6. Track Your Application Actively</h3>
      <p>Do not passively wait. After your application has been registered (week 2), contact the consulate monthly to confirm it is progressing. A simple status check can sometimes identify issues early and prevent delays.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

