      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>How long does a Non-Lucrative Visa Spain take to process?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The typical processing time for a <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-spain/">Non-Lucrative Visa Spain</a> ranges from 6 to 12 weeks from the date you submit your application at the consulate. However, this varies significantly by consulate location. Some consulates (Manchester, Miami) may process applications in 4–8 weeks, while others (New York, Sydney) can take 6–14 weeks. Incomplete applications or high consulate workload can extend timelines beyond 12 weeks.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is the fastest consulate for Non-Lucrative Visa processing?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Manchester and Miami consulates typically process NLV applications faster than others, with average times of 4–8 weeks. London averages 6–10 weeks, and New York 6–12 weeks. Processing times can change seasonally and depend on application volume. It is always best to check the specific consulate's website for their current processing times before you apply.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What happens if my NLV application is delayed beyond the expected timeline?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>If your application exceeds the typical processing timeframe, contact the consulate directly to check its status. Request a case reference number at the time of submission to track your application. Delays often occur due to background checks, additional document requests, or high application volumes. While waiting, do not book travel until you have received your visa decision. Your passport application office or visa service provider can sometimes obtain status updates on your behalf.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How quickly must I enter Spain after receiving my NLV visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Once approved, your <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-spain/">Non-Lucrative Visa</a> typically remains valid for 90 days from the date of issue. You must physically enter Spain within this 90-day window. The visa is activated (and the 1-year residence period begins) only when you cross the Spanish border. There is no grace period after the 90 days expire—if you do not enter by the deadline, you will need to reapply.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What do I do immediately after arriving in Spain on my NLV visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Upon arrival in Spain, you must apply for the Foreigner Identity Card (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero, or TIE) within 30 days of entry. Additionally, register your address on the municipal population register (padrón) in your local town hall within the same 30-day window. These are legal requirements. Both the TIE and padrón registration are essential for accessing healthcare, banking, employment verification, and residency proof in Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How do I apply for the TIE after arriving in Spain on the Non-Lucrative Visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You must apply for the TIE at your local National Police office (Policía Nacional) or through an authorized office (BLS or similar service provider). Bring your passport, entry stamp, completed application form (EX-15), two passport photos, and proof of address. The TIE typically takes 1–3 weeks to issue once you submit your application. You will receive a provisional certificate (resguardo) immediately, which serves as temporary proof of your legal status until the physical TIE card is ready for collection.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is a realistic worst-case timeline for the NLV process?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>In a worst case — slow consulate, document complications, requests for additional information — the total process from starting document preparation to arriving in Spain could take 6-8 months. This includes: 4-6 weeks for criminal records, 3-4 weeks for apostilles, 4-6 weeks for translations and medical certificate, 4-10 weeks for consulate appointment plus processing.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I start living in Spain before my NLV is approved?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>As a tourist (within the 90/180 Schengen rules), you can visit Spain while your NLV application is being processed. However, you cannot officially start your Spanish residence until the NLV is issued and you re-enter Spain on it. Many applicants visit Spain during processing to find accommodation or get affairs in order.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to get the TIE card after arriving on the NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>After arriving in Spain, you have 30 days to apply for your TIE card. The TIE appointment itself must be booked (allow 1-4 weeks for an appointment). After the biometric appointment, the physical card typically takes 1-4 weeks to be ready for collection. Total TIE process: allow 6-8 weeks after arrival.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is a realistic worst-case NLV timeline?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
